          %p
            the ‘pushout’ of this diagram is the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            obtained by taking the disjoint union
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A + B
            and identifying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in B
            if there exists
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in C
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(x) = b
            (and all identifications that follow to keep
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            an
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
          %p
            This construction comes up, for example, when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the intersection of the sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            are the obvious inclusions. Then the pushout is just the union of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            Note that there are maps
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_A : A \to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_B : B \to X
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_A(a) = [a]
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_B(b) = [b]
            respectively. These maps make this square commute:
